  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=-6,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x}?$",
  "worked_solution": "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4)=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x} & =\\frac{(-6)^{2}}{6\\cdot 4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
